				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/thopter" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(7156531); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=7156531&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1609055496" alt="Thopter" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=7156531)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/thopter" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(7156531); return false;" >Thopter</a></span> <span class="odate time_1609052730 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">27 Dec 2020 07:05</span>
																	<br/>
						in discussion <a href="/forum/c-89000/help:ideas-critique">Writing Help / Help: Ideas Critique</a> &raquo;
						<a href="/forum/t-13960972/a-teleporting-bridge">A teleporting bridge</a>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4854720">
									

<p>A harmless-but-Veil-endangering Keter SCP that is semi-contained in a probabilistic fashion. It's a bridge. It teleports. It's a teleporting bridge. It will teleport itself over any running water where a bridge could be. It aggravates the Foundation because it's impossible to guarantee its containment and its anomalous movement obviously violates the rules of normal reality, but at the same time, it's not really hurting anyone.</p>
<p><em>I moved and trimmed your thread summary, since it was taking up a lot of space in the main forum screen. Try to keep thread descriptions (the text in the little box under the thread title) short so you don't crowd out other authors in the forum listing. ~Zyn</em></p>
<p>My goal is to write a SCP with a conventional/classic Series I feel, while meeting modern standards. Here are the design goals I set for myself. You can skip reading 'em if you want to jump ahead to the idea itself.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p><strong>Design goals:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>The SCP writeup does not rely on any variety of format screw. (It's my first SCP, and I'd like to demonstrate I can play by the rules before bending them.)</li>
<li>The anomaly unquestionably breaks the Veil, blatantly violates normalcy, etc. There's absolutely no reasonable way to explain away the anomaly if one witnesses its anomalous properties in action.</li>
<li>Containment is <em>only</em> important because of the Foundation's mission. There's no violence, no danger, or only highly situational incidental danger. In part this is because I want to go for a <em>weird</em> vibe with this SCP, not a <em>scary</em> vibe; I want the reader to be curious, intrigued, but not horrified (at least not from a casual readthrough).</li>
<li>The anomaly is hard to contain. If I posted the description without the containment procedure, people would struggle to think up a containment solution. (I mean, okay, an audience of SCP readers/writers would probably think up a solution in five minutes&#8230; but they'd feel at least a little proud of themselves.)</li>
<li>The anomaly itself is simple. I can summarize the core idea in a few words. The hard-to-contain nature of the anomaly is not the result of tacking on lots of incredibly detailed complexity.</li>
<li>The anomaly is not sentient. I want an anomaly that plays by simple rules and can't adapt to complications.</li>
<li>The containment procedure is odd, it makes the reader scrunch their eyebrows and wonder what the hell is being contained. (As y'all know, this is a consequence of the format: it's necessary because of the order in which SCPs present information. I could write more of a classic puzzle if the description came before the containment procedure.)</li>
<li>The containment procedure is clearly not arbitrary, and is not symbolic/ritualistic. Even without knowing what the anomaly is (yet), the reader can intuit from the containment procedure that there's a concrete reason for the procedure.</li>
<li>The containment procedure is easily summarized, but the details are important. Once someone reads the description, they will hopefully think, &quot;ohhh, I see why they were doing that now.&quot; This is the main payoff the SCP provides: un-scrunched eyebrows, curiosity satisfied.</li>
<li>The containment procedure doesn't require having agents infiltrate some other organization, and having agents embedded in all the government organizations doesn't solve the containment problem.</li>
<li>The containment procedure doesn't cost too much, in manpower or money. I figure three or four skilled people, a few dozen unskilled workers, and capital/supplies roughly equivalent to what a business with that many employees might have.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Getting to the idea, thought process, etc:</strong><br />
After some tinkering and aimless brainstorming, I decided it would be fun to try to pay homage to one of the first SCPs I ever read, SCP-106. To avoid being derivative, I decided to pick just one aspect of that anomaly to emulate. There are two aspects of SCP-106 that make it very hard to contain: the old man's corrosive acid, and his limited ability to teleport/passwall. A teleporting thing seemed more open-ended than an acidic thing, so I chose teleportation as the aspect to emulate. My scip is hard to contain because it teleports. Okay, pretty basic and common, lots of scips teleport; maybe I can add aesthetic flourishes to gesture in the direction of SCP-106.</p>
<p>Teleportation is more interesting when it's limited. The old man's passwall-teleportation is limited in that he has to emerge from the same thing he passed into. There's a kind of ontological ambiguity in what counts as a thing, but in particular, I thought about how he can get through barriers that would normally impede movement. He gets past the solid obstructing him, and to the other side. That's not the only way the old man can teleport, but it's the one I chose as inspiration.</p>
<p>SCP-106 traps people. I decided to pick a thing that would normally do the opposite, and enable people to get places. A car, maybe? Nah, a vehicle felt too confining, and vehicles require drivers, which complicates how people can interact with the scip. In my head I imagined the hypothetical person who would use the scip as travelling on foot, for whatever reason, maybe because the people fleeing SCP-106 are generally on foot. So, okay, walking&#8230; a teleporting airport moving walkway seemed delightfully weird, and the idea of a thing blipping from airport to airport instantaneously but unpredictably felt like it had potential to be a funny commentary on airports and travel&#8230; but eh, that also felt too silly for the design goals, and airports are so specific. (I might eventually adapt this idea into something, though&#8230;) A teleporting path would also be cool, but path seemed so general, I couldn't think of a non-arbitrary limitation. As a kind of in-between, why not a bridge? There are inherent limitations to where a bridge can teleport; it should connect two land masses with water in between and let you walk from one to the other without stepping in the water. Since this bridge is anomalous, it can adapt its size to whatever waterway or body of water it crosses. People can use the bridge to get past the liquid obstructing them, and to the other side.</p>
<p><strong>The idea itself (with a bit of detail):</strong><br />
So, it's a bridge that teleports. As with SCP-106, I have to handwave the limitations a little bit; I don't want to get too bogged down thinking about how to specify that it can't end up over a bathtub or between skyscrapers. It can only end up in places where a bridge would make sense. It stretches or shrinks to fit whatever water it ends up over. Maybe it crosses a tiny brook in the middle of nowhere, maybe it crosses a mighty river. (Allowing it to cross standing water introduces complications, because at that point it could cross the Pacific Ocean or whatever. Requiring a current limits the scope of the bridge. Rivers only get so wide.) Fixed width, I guess, say roughly 1 meter, so that a person can walk over it, and gentle curved with whatever maximum height lets it remain consistently above the water. I'll handwave thickness and mass. It should appear to be made out of something cool, like obsidian.</p>
<p>The bridge teleports whenever it comes into contact with any liquid or solid other than the ground its ends rest upon (this includes loose earth; it can't just be picked up by digging under each end without triggering its teleportation). I doubt too much detail on its design history will make it into the SCP writeup, but I imagine the bridge was designed to be water-repellent and grime-repellent, i.e. it was <em>supposed</em> to anomalously displace liquids and solids it came into contact with via teleportation. The unspoken joke will be that, instead of a hydrophobic bridge, the designer created a bridge with hydrophobia.</p>
<p>What do you all think? I have ideas for both failed containment procedure attempts as well as the eventual mostly-successful containment procedure that is implemented as of the writing of the SCP, but I'm not sure if I'm supposed to put those here.</p>
</div>
